{
  "component": "Radio Field",
  "description": "A single radio choice with a label and an optional description line, built as eight variants covering four states and two value types. Checked and Unchecked exist as a design-file property only; in code the state comes from the group value.\n\nPURPOSE: Lets a person pick one option from a set of mutually exclusive choices. Each instance is one option, with supporting description text underneath the label for extra context.\n\nBEHAVIOUR: Four states are drawn: Default, Hover, Focus and Disabled. Hover signals pointer targeting, Focus adds a focus ring using Stroke/Focus Ring and Border/Focus/Default, and Disabled swaps all fills, borders, icon and text to the disabled token set. Selection is not toggled by the component itself. Radio fields are grouped in code and the group value decides which one renders as checked.\n\nCOMPOSITION: No nested component instances were detected. Each variant is built from plain frames and text layers: a \"Checkbox and Label\" row holding the \"Radio\" frame and the \"Label\" text, then a \"Description Row\" holding a \"Space\" spacer frame and the \"Description\" text. AI code generators should still check the development codebase for an existing radio input, label and helper text primitive before writing new ones.\n\nUSAGE: Use for one-of-many selection where all options should be visible at once. Use a checkbox field when more than one choice can be selected, and a select or dropdown when the list is long. Turn Has Description off when the label alone is clear enough, so the spacer row does not add dead space.\n\nCODE GENERATION NOTES: Render a native input of type radio with a shared name attribute per group, and bind the label with a for or wrapping label element. Attach the description with aria-describedby. Do not model Checked as a prop on the field itself; derive it from the group value. Hover and Focus should come from CSS pseudo-classes rather than separate components, and Focus should use :focus-visible so the ring only shows for keyboard users. Disabled must set the disabled attribute, not just the disabled colour tokens. Arrow key roving focus within the group is standard radio behaviour and is not visible in the design file.",
